      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To convert rectangular coordinates $(x, y, z)$ to spherical coordinates $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$, we use the following formulas:\n\n1. $\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}$\n2. $\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)$\n3. $\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right)$\n\nGiven the point $(0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)$, we can substitute the given values into these formulas.\n\nStarting with $\\rho$:\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2} = \\sqrt{0^2 + (-3\\sqrt{3})^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{0 + 27 + 9} = \\sqrt{36} = 6\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate $\\theta$:\n\\[\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}}{0}\\right)\n\\]\nSince the point $(0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)$ is on the negative $y$-axis, $\\theta$ must be $\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$.\n\nFinally, we calculate $\\phi$:\n\\[\n\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right) = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{6}\\right) = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates for the point $(0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)$ are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(6, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)}\n\\]",
